---
description: Восстановить пропущенное тестовое покрытие и проверить текущее изменение ITL
agent: code
---

Use this command only in an active `itldev/*` worktree after skipped YAxUnit/Vanessa coverage or failed complete verification.

Begin one helper-owned repair session. Keep its id; it permits at most `ITL_VERIFICATION_REPAIR_MAX_ATTEMPTS` (default `5`) unfiltered runs. Do not pass `VanessaFeaturePath` or `VanessaFilterTags`:

```powershell
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\.agents\skills\1c-workflow\scripts\run-itl-command.ps1 -- -Action begin-verification-repair
```

Use the session id from compact `userReport`.

1. Identify the current agent-made configuration/extension change from the request, Git diff and configured roots. A master merge alone is not agent-made behavior. For classification, read the inventory; unchanged legacy BSL is exempt.
2. Read `.agents/skills/1c-workflow/references/verification-result.md`. Use JUnit, helper/1C/event logs and owned process evidence to distinguish runner or environment, test fixture, and product failure before editing. An assertion or missing UI element needs requirement and runtime evidence.
3. For a proven product defect, choose YAxUnit for local algorithmic decisions, Vanessa for object interaction or UI, or both when each proves a different risk. Search existing tests first: if sufficient, reuse it unchanged; otherwise strengthen one or add the smallest missing regression. Resolve new BSL applicability per `.agents/skills/1c-workflow/references/verification-suite-selection.md`; do not add or edit a test merely because this command was invoked. Read `.agents/skills/1c-workflow/references/vanessa-tests.md` before authoring a scenario; use UI MCP only for actual UI/step evidence.
4. Run the compact helper from this project with `timeout_ms >= 3900000`; raise it if Designer can exceed 3600 seconds. A short outer timeout can interrupt 1C:

```powershell
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\.agents\skills\1c-workflow\scripts\run-itl-command.ps1 -- -Action check-dev-branch -VerificationTrigger repair -RepairSessionId <session-id>
```

The helper owns the branch infobase update, both test layers, `TESTMANAGER -> TESTCLIENT`, JUnit/log evidence, and event-log baseline check. Do not update the base separately.

On failure, diagnose again. Fix a defective scenario only when requirement evidence proves it wrong; fix the implementation for a product defect. Apply the conditional post-failure runtime diagnostic from `vanessa-authoring.md` before an evidence-dependent UI edit or the next full attempt; it is not an extra gate or repair attempt. Never delete, skip, filter or weaken the core assertion for a green run. Batch edits with targeted/static checks; rerun the full helper at a decision milestone or after the final relevant edit, not after every small edit. Reuse this session. At the configured maximum the helper refuses another full run: return blocker diagnostics. After a pass, resume the original task.

On fresh pass, report cause, chosen layer, reason for both if needed, reused or added tests, base update, applicable YAxUnit/Vanessa evidence and event-log result. Keep one compact result, not a report per module.
